1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:07,167
The buses come every ten minutes, on average. So if you show
up at a random time, how long should you wait?

2
00:00:07,333 --> 00:00:11,567
Five minutes? That answer feels obvious. And it can be wrong.

3
00:00:11,867 --> 00:00:15,667
If a bus arrives exactly every ten minutes, you are right.

4
00:00:15,833 --> 00:00:22,933
Arrive just before a bus, and you barely wait. Arrive just
after one, and you wait almost ten minutes.

5
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:28,067
For arrivals spread evenly across time, those waits average
five minutes.

6
00:00:28,367 --> 00:00:31,867
But now keep the same average and change the pattern.

7
00:00:32,033 --> 00:00:36,800
One gap is two minutes. The next is eighteen. Then the pattern
repeats.

8
00:00:36,967 --> 00:00:42,033
Two plus eighteen, divided by two. The average gap is still
ten minutes.

9
00:00:42,333 --> 00:00:48,367
Here is the trap: you are not choosing a gap at random. You
are choosing a moment in time.

10
00:00:48,533 --> 00:00:54,667
An eighteen-minute gap gives you nine times as much time to
land in it as a two-minute gap.

11
00:00:54,833 --> 00:01:00,733
Imagine twenty people arriving, one per minute, halfway
through each minute of this cycle.

12
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:04,733
Two land in the short gap. Eighteen land in the long one.

13
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:10,833
Half the gaps are long. But ninety percent of these passengers
experience a long gap.

14
00:01:11,133 --> 00:01:14,733
Inside the short gap, the average wait is one minute.

15
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:17,800
Inside the long gap, it is nine minutes.

16
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:25,433
Weight those waits by where people actually arrive: ten
percent times one, plus ninety percent times nine.

17
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:32,967
The result is eight point two minutes. Not five. Same average
bus gap. A very different average wait.

18
00:01:33,267 --> 00:01:39,767
You can see why on this graph. Right after a bus leaves, your
wait for the next one is at its highest.

19
00:01:39,933 --> 00:01:44,233
As the next bus approaches, the remaining wait falls to zero.

20
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:50,033
A longer gap makes the triangle wider and taller. That adds
much more waiting time.

21
00:01:50,333 --> 00:01:59,333
This is the inspection paradox. When you inspect a process at
a random time, longer intervals are more likely to be the ones
you find.

22
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:08,967
It does not mean every bus is late. Our example is a
simplified repeating pattern, with passengers arriving
independently of the schedule.

23
00:02:09,133 --> 00:02:15,467
If you time your arrival using a timetable or a live tracker,
that is a different way of sampling.

24
00:02:15,767 --> 00:02:24,000
The lesson is bigger than buses: before trusting an average,
ask what is being averaged, and how your experience is
selected.

25
00:02:24,167 --> 00:02:27,767
The timetable counts gaps. Your day samples time.

26
00:02:27,967 --> 00:02:29,467
Smarter choices.

27
00:02:29,767 --> 00:02:31,200
A better future.

28
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:32,933
Follow for more.
